::ALG2_U03_UA_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify &radic;50. {
= 5&radic;2#Correct. 50 = 25*2 and sqrt(25)=5.
~ 25&radic;2#The square root of 25 is 5.
~ 10&radic;5#This does not square back to 50.
~ 2&radic;25#The perfect square factor should be rooted.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite x<sup>3/4</sup> in radical form. {
~ &radic;<sup>3</sup>(x<sup>4</sup>)#This reverses numerator and denominator.
= &radic;<sup>4</sup>(x<sup>3</sup>)#Correct. The denominator 4 is the root index and the numerator 3 is the power.
~ 4x<sup>3</sup>#A rational exponent is not multiplication by 4.
~ &radic;(x<sup>3</sup>)#This uses square root instead of fourth root.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 27<sup>2/3</sup>. {
~ 6#This multiplies numerator and denominator.
~ 18#This does not apply the cube root.
= 9#Correct. The cube root of 27 is 3, and 3 squared is 9.
~ 3#This stops after taking the cube root.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the domain of f(x)=&radic;(x+4)? {
~ x &le; -4#This allows negative radicands to the left.
~ x &ne; -4#x = -4 is allowed.
~ all real numbers#Inputs less than -4 are not allowed.
= x &ge; -4#Correct. The radicand must be nonnegative.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the range of f(x)=&radic;x - 3? {
= y &ge; -3#Correct. The square root output starts at 0, then shifts down 3.
~ y &ge; 3#The graph shifts down, not up.
~ x &ge; -3#Range describes output values.
~ all real numbers#A square root graph has a minimum output here.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Solve &radic;(x-2)=4. {
~ x = 2#This makes the radical 0.
= x = 18#Correct. Squaring gives x - 2 = 16.
~ x = 14#This subtracts 2 instead of adding it.
~ x = 4#This is the radical value, not x.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which value checks for &radic;(x+9)=x-3? {
~ x = 0 only#x = 0 gives 3 = -3, which is false.
~ x = 0 and x = 7#x = 0 is extraneous.
= x = 7 only#Correct. x = 0 appears after squaring but fails the original equation.
~ no solution#x = 7 checks.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3<br><br>Question:<br>A model d=&radic;(16t) has d=20. What is t? {
~ t = 5#This divides 20 by 4 before squaring.
~ t = 20#This is the distance value.
~ t = 400#This is d squared, not t.
= t = 25#Correct. 400 = 16t, so t = 25.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x<sup>2</sup>-9)/(x-3). {
= x + 3, x &ne; 3#Correct. Factor as (x-3)(x+3) and keep the original restriction.
~ x - 3, x &ne; 3#The remaining factor is x+3.
~ x + 3, no restriction#The original denominator excludes x=3.
~ x<sup>2</sup>+3#The numerator must be factored.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>What restriction applies to 4/(x+5)? {
~ x &ne; 5#The sign is reversed.
= x &ne; -5#Correct. The denominator is zero at x=-5.
~ x &ne; 4#The numerator does not restrict the domain.
~ all real numbers#One input makes the denominator zero.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Multiply (2x/3)(9/x<sup>2</sup>). {
~ 6x#One x remains in the denominator.
~ 3/x#The coefficient simplifies to 6.
= 6/x, x &ne; 0#Correct. The product is 18x/(3x^2)=6/x.
~ 6/x, no restriction#The original denominator excludes x=0.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 2/x + 5/x. {
~ 7/(2x)#Do not add matching denominators.
~ 10/x#The numerators add, not multiply.
~ 7/x, no restriction#The denominator excludes x=0.
= 7/x, x &ne; 0#Correct. Common denominators stay the same while numerators add.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 1/x + 1/(x+2). {
= (2x+2)/(x(x+2)), x &ne; 0 and x &ne; -2#Correct. Use LCD x(x+2) and combine x+2+x.
~ 2/(2x+2)#Denominators are not added.
~ (x+2)/(x(x+2))#The second numerator contributes x.
~ (2x+2)/(x+2)#The full LCD remains.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.4<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 6/x = 3. {
~ x = 0#This is restricted.
= x = 2#Correct. Multiply by x to get 6=3x.
~ x = 1/2#This is the reciprocal.
~ x = 6#This gives 1, not 3.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.4<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 2/(x-1)=1. {
~ x = 1#This is restricted.
~ x = -3#This does not check.
= x = 3#Correct. Multiply by x-1 to get 2=x-1.
~ x = 2#This gives 2, not 1.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which value is restricted in 1/(x-8)=2? {
~ x = -8#The sign is reversed.
~ x = 2#This is not a denominator zero.
~ x = 0#The denominator is not zero at 0.
= x = 8#Correct. The denominator x-8 cannot be zero.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6<br><br>Question:<br>What is the vertical asymptote of f(x)=1/(x+6)? {
= x = -6#Correct. Set x+6=0.
~ x = 6#The sign is reversed.
~ y = -6#Vertical asymptotes use x=value.
~ y = 0#This is horizontal behavior.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6<br><br>Question:<br>What is the horizontal asymptote of f(x)=5/x? {
~ x = 0#That is the vertical asymptote.
= y = 0#Correct. A constant over x approaches 0 as x grows.
~ y = 5#The output does not approach 5.
~ x = 5#This is not an asymptote.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6<br><br>Question:<br>What is the horizontal asymptote of f(x)=(2x+1)/(x-4)? {
~ x = 4#That is vertical asymptote.
~ y = 0#Equal degrees do not approach 0 here.
= y = 2#Correct. Equal degrees use the leading coefficient ratio 2/1.
~ y = 1#This uses a constant.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6<br><br>Question:<br>In T(n)=90/n, why is n=0 not allowed? {
~ T equals 90#90/0 is undefined.
~ T equals 0#Division by zero is not 0.
~ n=0 is the maximum#It is not in the domain.
= division by zero is undefined and zero objects is not meaningful#Correct. The denominator and context both reject n=0.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify &radic;98. {
= 7&radic;2#Correct. 98=49*2 and sqrt(49)=7.
~ 49&radic;2#sqrt(49)=7, not 49.
~ 14&radic;7#This does not square back to 98.
~ 2&radic;49#The perfect square should be rooted.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 16<sup>3/4</sup>. {
~ 4#This stops at the fourth root squared incorrectly.
= 8#Correct. The fourth root of 16 is 2 and 2^3=8.
~ 12#This multiplies 3 and 4.
~ 64#This cubes 4 instead of the fourth root.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the starting point of f(x)=&radic;(x-2)+5? {
~ (-2, 5)#The sign inside is reversed.
~ (2, -5)#The vertical shift is up 5.
= (2, 5)#Correct. The square root starts when x-2=0, then y=5.
~ (0, 5)#The radicand starts at x=2.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Solve &radic;(3x+1)=4. {
~ x = 3#This does not make the radicand 16.
~ x = 15#This misses division by 3.
~ x = 17#This adds instead of solving.
= x = 5#Correct. Squaring gives 3x+1=16.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x<sup>2</sup>+4x)/(x). {
= x + 4, x &ne; 0#Correct. Factor x(x+4) and keep the original restriction.
~ x + 4, no restriction#The denominator excludes x=0.
~ x<sup>2</sup>+4#The common factor x should cancel.
~ 4#Canceling only one term is invalid.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q26::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q26<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 3/(x+1) - 1/(x+1). {
~ 4/(x+1)#The operation is subtraction.
= 2/(x+1), x &ne; -1#Correct. Subtract numerators over the common denominator.
~ 2/(2x+2)#The denominator is not doubled.
~ 2/(x+1), no restriction#The denominator excludes x=-1.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q27::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q27<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.4<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x/(x-2)=3. {
~ x = 2#This is restricted.
~ x = -3#This does not check.
= x = 3#Correct. Multiply by x-2 to get x=3x-6.
~ x = 6#This is 2x, not x.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q28::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q28<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6<br><br>Question:<br>What is the domain of f(x)=(x+1)/(x-7)? {
~ x &ne; -1#The numerator zero is allowed.
~ x &ne; -7#The sign is reversed.
~ all real numbers#One value is restricted.
= x &ne; 7#Correct. The denominator is zero at x=7.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q29::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q29<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6<br><br>Question:<br>What is the y-intercept of f(x)=3/(x+1)? {
= (0, 3)#Correct. Substitute x=0 to get 3.
~ (-1, 0)#x=-1 is restricted.
~ (0, 1)#The output is 3.
~ (3, 0)#This switches input and output.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q30::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q30<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3<br><br>Question:<br>What makes a radical equation solution extraneous? {
~ it is a fraction#Fractions can be valid.
= it fails the original equation#Correct. A candidate from squaring must check in the original equation.
~ it is positive#Positive values can be valid.
~ it uses a radical symbol#Radical notation can be valid.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q31::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q31<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.4<br><br>Question:<br>What makes a rational equation solution invalid? {
~ it is negative#Negative values can be valid.
~ it is a fraction#Fractions can be valid.
= it makes an original denominator zero#Correct. Restrictions from original denominators must be honored.
~ it was found after clearing denominators#That process is valid if restrictions are checked.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q32::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q32<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which parent function has domain x >= 0 and range y >= 0? {
~ f(x)=1/x#That rational function excludes x=0.
~ f(x)=x<sup>2</sup>#A quadratic has all real domain.
~ f(x)=x<sup>3</sup>#A cubic has all real domain and range.
= f(x)=&radic;x#Correct. The square root parent starts at the origin and extends right/up.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q33::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q33<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>2</sup>-1)/(x+2) by (x-1)/(x+2). {
= x + 1, x &ne; -2 and x &ne; 1#Correct. Multiply by the reciprocal and keep restrictions.
~ x - 1#The remaining factor is x+1.
~ x + 1, no restriction#Original denominators and divisor create restrictions.
~ 1/(x+1)#This flips the result.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q34::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q34<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which rational function has vertical asymptote x=-3? {
~ f(x)=1/(x-3)#This has asymptote x=3.
= f(x)=1/(x+3)#Correct. The denominator is zero at x=-3.
~ f(x)=x+3#This is linear.
~ f(x)=3/x#This has asymptote x=0.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q35::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q35<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3<br><br>Question:<br>A model d=&radic;(25t) has d=15. What is t? {
~ t = 3#This divides 15 by 5 before squaring.
~ t = 15#This is the output value.
= t = 9#Correct. 225=25t, so t=9.
~ t = 225#This is d squared.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q36::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q36<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 1/(x-1)+1/(x+1). {
~ 2/((x-1)(x+1))#The x terms add to 2x.
~ 2/(2x)#Denominators are not added.
~ 2x/(x+1)#The full LCD remains.
= 2x/((x-1)(x+1)), x &ne; 1 and x &ne; -1#Correct. The numerator is (x+1)+(x-1)=2x.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q37::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q37<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite &radic;<sup>3</sup>(x<sup>2</sup>) using rational exponents. {
= x<sup>2/3</sup>#Correct. Cube root gives denominator 3 and power 2 gives numerator 2.
~ x<sup>3/2</sup>#This reverses index and power.
~ 2x<sup>1/3</sup>#The 2 is an exponent, not a coefficient.
~ x<sup>1/6</sup>#The exponents are not multiplied this way.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q38::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q38<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6<br><br>Question:<br>What does a vertical asymptote represent in a rational model? {
~ the initial output#Initial output is f(0).
= an input the model approaches but cannot use#Correct. The denominator restriction creates an input where the model is undefined.
~ the maximum output always#An asymptote is not necessarily a maximum.
~ the x-intercept#An x-intercept is where output is 0.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q39::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q39<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.4<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 10/(x+3)=2. {
~ x = -3#This is restricted.
~ x = 5#This gives 10/8.
= x = 2#Correct. 10=2(x+3), so x=2.
~ x = 8#This is not the solved value.
}

::ALG2_U03_UA_Q40::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_UA_Q40<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which check is most important in Unit 3 equation solving? {
~ ignore restrictions after simplifying#Restrictions remain important.
~ use only decimal answers#Exact forms are often needed.
~ choose the largest candidate#Size does not determine validity.
= verify candidates in the original equation and restrictions#Correct. Radical and rational equations can create invalid candidates.
}

